# Water-operations mission parameters shared by SITL and REAL (Copter 4.7).
# Ordered application: drone profile, operating-mode profile, this mission.
# No SIM_* settings or hardware backend selection belong in this shared layer.
# Parameters authorize neither arming nor LAND. Native readiness, existing FSM
# checks and coordinator permissions remain mandatory. Retry -> RTL is pending.
# REAL upload must read existing values first, require fresh native ground/disarm,
# verify actual readback, and never reboot/arm/change mode on the user's behalf.

# FS_GCS_TIMEOUT -- GCS/telemetry-heartbeat loss timeout, seconds.
# Upstream metadata: "Timeout before triggering the GCS failsafe"; allowed range
# is 2..120 s in 1 s increments. ArduCopter measures elapsed time since the last
# MAVLink heartbeat from the device whose system ID matches SYSID_MYGCS. Eight
# seconds was operator-selected to tolerate short telemetry interruptions without
# immediately declaring the GCS link lost. This value does not itself enable the
# failsafe or select its response: FS_GCS_ENABLE controls whether loss acts and
# chooses RTL/Land/other behavior, while FS_OPTIONS may permit continuation in
# particular flight modes. The currently observed FS_GCS_ENABLE=0 means this
# timeout remains dormant until GCS failsafe is deliberately enabled. It is not
# the RC receiver failsafe timeout (RC_FS_TIMEOUT), a MOOS-health watchdog, or a
# guarantee that pArduBridge and the rest of MOOS are healthy merely because a
# MAVLink heartbeat is present.
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#fs-gcs-timeout
# Source: ArduCopter/Parameters.cpp and events.cpp in companion
# ardupilot-sitl/ardupilot (reviewed Copter 4.7.1).
FS_GCS_TIMEOUT    8

# RTL_ALT_M -- baseline minimum RTL return altitude, metres (Copter 4.7).
# Upstream metadata: "The minimum alt above home the vehicle will climb to
# before returning. If the vehicle is flying higher than this value it will
# return at its current altitude." User-approved 15 m matches the default
# task-frozen rally height; this is not a sensor-height or deck-clearance value.
# With user-approved RTL_ALT_TYPE=0 (Home-relative frame), 15 means 15 m
# above FC Home, not above water, terrain or the target. Legacy RTL_ALT uses
# cm: its equivalent would be 1500, NOT 15; this uses the 4.7 metre name.
# Rally altitude and current altitude/minimum climb participate in the return
# calculation. RTL_CONE_SLOPE is a dimensionless height/distance ratio limiting
# the initial climb, not a 10 m arrival setpoint or continuous approach descent.
# Cone is explicitly disabled below; fence settings remain inherited. Minimum
# additional climb is zero. Return altitude can still differ from this baseline.
# User-selected behavior: acquire range/target during final RTL landing descent,
# rather than forcing a 10 m return or final hold. This entry does not change
# RTL_ALT_FINAL_M, authorize LAND, start RTL after retry exhaustion, or guarantee
# rangefinder health/target acquisition. A 12 m sensor may be out of range at
# return height; the existing PLND_ALT_MAX=4 search exception permits descent
# above 4 m. Missing/unhealthy height still bypasses strict protection upstream.
# Official RTL behavior: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/rtl-mode.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rtl-alt-m
# Pinned calculation: ArduCopter/mode_rtl.cpp, ModeRTL::compute_return_target
# https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/dbe792162d06cab66c3475fd5556bf7a120f119e/ArduCopter/mode_rtl.cpp
RTL_ALT_M         15

# RTL_ALT_TYPE -- RTL altitude reference (enum, no physical units).
# Upstream choices: "0:Relative to Home, 1:Terrain". User-approved 0 keeps
# RTL_ALT_M=15 and the frozen rally's default altitude Home-relative. Home is
# the FC's reference, not a continuously tracked boat/deck or terrain surface.
# Choose 0 to avoid making return navigation depend on terrain data or on the
# 12 m downward rangefinder, which cannot support a 15 m rangefinder-terrain
# return baseline. For enum 1, WP_RFND_USE selects rangefinder (1) or terrain
# database (0); terrain-source availability/fallback needs separate review.
# This entry does not change WP_RFND_USE or disable the downward sensor.
# Precision landing still uses native rangefinder height for PLND_ALT_MIN/MAX,
# independently of the Home-relative return frame. Target acquisition is
# intended during final descent, not guaranteed at the return altitude.
# A fixed Home-relative altitude is not an obstacle/terrain-clearance guarantee;
# current height, rally, cone, minimum climb and fence constraints still apply.
# This is upstream behavior, not a custom retry-to-RTL policy or flight command.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/rtl-mode.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rtl-alt-type
# Source: ArduCopter/Parameters.cpp and ModeRTL::compute_return_target in
# companion ardupilot-sitl/ardupilot (reviewed Copter 4.7.1).
RTL_ALT_TYPE      0

# RTL_CLIMB_MIN_M -- minimum additional initial RTL climb, metres (Copter 4.7).
# Upstream metadata: "The vehicle will climb this many meters during the
# initial climb portion of the RTL" (documented range 0..30 m).
# User-selected 0 means no mandatory extra climb relative to current height.
# It does NOT disable the RTL_ALT_M=15 baseline, request descent during return,
# or cap RTL at 15 m: current height and the chosen rally still participate.
# With cone disabled and no restrictive fence, an 8 m starting height returns
# at 15 m; a 20 m starting height returns at 20 m rather than adding 2 m.
# Cone is explicitly disabled below; fence constraints still apply. Zero is
# not an obstacle-clearance/terrain-following guarantee or final-descent hold.
# Final landing/search descent is separate; this parameter neither starts RTL
# after precision retry exhaustion nor grants LAND/arming authority.
# This is upstream behavior, not an external-ship or recovery-policy patch.
# Legacy RTL_CLIMB_MIN is centimetres; use the 4.7 metre parameter here.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/rtl-mode.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rtl-climb-min-m
# Source: ArduCopter/mode_rtl.cpp, ModeRTL::var_info and compute_return_target
# in companion ardupilot-sitl/ardupilot (reviewed Copter 4.7.1).
RTL_CLIMB_MIN_M   0

# RTL_CONE_SLOPE -- initial RTL climb-limiting cone slope, dimensionless ratio
# of height to horizontal distance from the selected return destination.
# Upstream: "Defines a cone above home which determines maximum climb".
# Metadata range 0..10, increment 0.1; examples 0=Disabled, 1=Shallow, 3=Steep.
# User explicitly approved 0 (disabled) so being near the frozen rally does
# not lower the return climb merely because the horizontal distance is small.
# A nonzero active cone can reduce the calculated target altitude toward
# distance*slope, subject to the native current-height/minimum-climb floor.
# It is not a 10 m arrival height, vertical speed, angle in degrees, glide path
# or continuous descent command. Zero bypasses the cone reduction only.
# With RTL_ALT_TYPE=0, RTL_ALT_M=15, RTL_CLIMB_MIN_M=0, the frozen rally at
# Home+15 m and no restrictive altitude fence, an 8 m aircraft climbs to 15 m;
# a 20 m aircraft returns at 20 m. Higher rally altitude can raise the target;
# native fence limits and estimator vertical offsets still participate. This
# neither disables geofencing nor guarantees obstacle/deck/terrain clearance.
# Final search descent remains separate: after return and RTL_LOIT_TIME=10000,
# RTL_ALT_FINAL_M=0 selects native landing. Missing-target policy, range health
# and the explicitly permitted pilot-reposition override remain unchanged.
# No RTL request, verified-rally readiness or retry-exhaustion policy is added.
# This is upstream behavior, not a custom simulator/recovery patch.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/rtl-mode.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rtl-cone-slope
# Source: ArduCopter/Parameters.cpp and mode_rtl.cpp,
# ModeRTL::compute_return_target, reviewed companion Copter 4.7.1.
RTL_CONE_SLOPE    0

# RTL_LOIT_TIME -- pause over the RTL destination before final descent, ms.
# Upstream metadata: "Time (in milliseconds) to loiter above home before
# beginning final descent" (documented range 0..60000 ms). The destination
# may be the selected rally, despite the native state's "home" name.
# User-selected 10000 ms = 10 seconds, increasing the default 5 second pause
# to allow more settling time after arrival. The timer starts when native RTL
# enters its loiter stage after reaching the return waypoint, not at RTL entry.
# It uses the FC's millis() clock; SITL speedup can shorten host wall-clock
# duration. It is not a MOOS timeout or evidence of arrival/landing/readiness.
# Native RESET_TO_ARMED_YAW alignment, when active, must also reach its yaw
# tolerance; the phase can therefore last longer than 10 seconds. This setting
# neither requests a particular yaw nor changes WP_YAW_BEHAVIOR.
# Loiter remains at the calculated return altitude, not at a new 10 m height.
# At the 15 m baseline, a 12 m sensor/target may remain out of range throughout
# the pause. Waiting longer does not manufacture sensor health/acquisition:
# the selected search descent occurs afterward, subject to native RTL/landing
# state, final-altitude setting and precision policy. No target-readiness gate
# is added by this timer. This is upstream behavior, not retry-exhaustion -> RTL.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/rtl-mode.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rtl-loit-time
# Source: ArduCopter/Parameters.cpp and ModeRTL::loiterathome_start/run in
# companion ardupilot-sitl/ardupilot (reviewed Copter 4.7.1).
RTL_LOIT_TIME     10000

# RTL_SPEED_MS -- requested horizontal RTL return speed, metres/second (4.7).
# Upstream metadata: "The speed in m/s which the aircraft will attempt to
# maintain horizontally while flying home. If this is set to zero, WP_SPD
# will be used instead." Documented range 0..20 m/s; 0 is inheritance, not stop.
# User-approved 2 m/s selects a modest return speed for water-operations trials
# instead of inheriting a potentially faster waypoint speed. This is a native
# earth-frame navigation speed request, not an airspeed or exact-speed promise.
# Native acceleration/braking, available thrust, wind, navigation quality and
# distance to the return waypoint can change actual speed. 2 m/s is not proof
# of wind margin, obstacle clearance, target tracking or platform readiness.
# This changes the RTL waypoint controller's speed selection, not WP_SPD or
# MOOS task/route speed parameters. It does not select climb/descent speed,
# change the 10 second RTL loiter, require target acquisition during return,
# or add a 10 m altitude hold. Landing descent/precision behavior is separate.
# No RTL/flight command or custom retry-exhaustion recovery is issued here.
# Legacy RTL_SPEED uses cm/s: the equivalent is 200; use the metre/second name.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/rtl-mode.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rtl-speed-ms
# Source: ArduCopter/mode_rtl.cpp, ModeRTL::var_info/init; init passes speed_ms
# to wp_nav->wp_and_spline_init_m in the reviewed Copter 4.7.1 source.
RTL_SPEED_MS      2

# RTL_ALT_FINAL_M -- RTL final-altitude/landing choice, metres (Copter 4.7).
# Upstream metadata: "Altitude the vehicle will move to as the final stage
# of Returning to Launch or after completing a mission. Set to zero to land."
# User-approved 0 selects native RTL landing after return and the 10 second
# loiter, rather than descending to a positive Home-relative altitude and
# holding. Zero is the LAND selector, not a literal 0 m altitude setpoint:
# native touchdown detection and disarm logic own the final ground state.
# This does NOT require target acquisition before starting final descent.
# With PLND_ALT_MAX=4, search descent above 4 m is allowed. Retain the separately
# approved PLND_STRICT=2 and PLND_ALT_MIN=0: when height is healthy and in the
# strict region, never-seen targets can cause hover; acquired/lost targets use
# native loss delay/retries, then hover if unsuccessful. This is not immediate
# hover on every lost packet. Missing/unhealthy range data produces OUT_OF_RANGE
# and can permit descent even below 4 m; these parameters do not unconditionally
# guarantee target-required touchdown. Native sensor/height validation remains
# necessary. The same precision policy is used by RTL's native landing stage
# and ordinary LAND; this entry does not bypass coordinator AUTOLAND checks.
# It neither enters RTL, uploads/selects a rally, implements retry-exhaustion
# recovery, nor sends any mode/flight/reboot command. This is upstream behavior.
# Legacy RTL_ALT_FINAL uses cm; use the 4.7 metre name (0 has the same selector).
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/rtl-mode.html
# Precision policy: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rtl-alt-final-m
# Source: ArduCopter/mode_rtl.cpp, ModeRTL::var_info/build_path/land_run, and
# libraries/AC_PrecLand in reviewed companion ardupilot-sitl/ardupilot 4.7.1.
RTL_ALT_FINAL_M   0

# RTL_OPTIONS -- optional RTL behavior bitmask, no physical units.
# Reviewed upstream bit 2 = "Ignore pilot yaw", numeric mask 2^2 = 4.
# User approved leaving bit 2 UNSET; value 0 selects no optional RTL behavior
# in the reviewed firmware. It is not enum 2, a precision-landing switch, or
# a request for zero heading. Do not import undocumented bits from other versions.
# Thus valid pilot yaw input remains permitted during RTL return/loiter; choose
# normal intervention behavior rather than suppressing it. SITL normally has no
# RC input, so this does not invent a pilot/RC source or change --no-rc settings.
# Final-descent/LAND stages delegate pilot-yaw policy to native LAND instead;
# unset RTL bit 2 alone does not configure or guarantee landing-stage override.
# WP_YAW_BEHAVIOR separately controls automatic RTL/mission yaw. This does
# not issue yaw alignment, track PEARL's heading or enable continuous LAND yaw.
# Precision target acquisition/loss, native range validity, rally selection and
# retry budgets remain unchanged. No flight command or custom recovery patch.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/rtl-mode.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rtl-options
# Source: ArduCopter/Parameters.cpp and ModeRTL::use_pilot_yaw/option_is_enabled
# in mode_rtl.cpp, reviewed companion Copter 4.7.1.
RTL_OPTIONS       0

# WP_YAW_BEHAVIOR -- automatic mission/RTL yaw selection (enum, no units).
# Upstream choices: 0 "Never change yaw", 1 "Face next waypoint",
# 2 "Face next waypoint except RTL", 3 "Face along GPS course".
# User-approved 2 retains normal waypoint-facing mission behavior but selects
# native AutoYaw HOLD during RTL, instead of pointing at the return destination.
# The purpose is to preserve the heading already independently aligned with
# PEARL, not undo that alignment while returning to the frozen rally. Native
# RTL loiter also retains HOLD instead of its automatic reset-to-armed-yaw
# branch when the RTL default yaw is HOLD. This is not a specific heading value.
# It does not perform/verify alignment, continuously track PEARL's heading,
# issue CONDITION_YAW, guarantee alignment if the boat turns, or change the
# coordinator's fresh post-ACK yaw admission. Normal mission waypoint legs
# can still change yaw; explicit flight commands/mode changes have their own
# rules. This is not a blanket fixed-heading guarantee across every mode.
# RTL_OPTIONS bit 2 remains unset, so valid pilot yaw can still intervene in
# RTL return/loiter; final-descent/LAND pilot-yaw policy is separate. Maintaining
# ship alignment therefore still requires native measured yaw and supervision,
# not treating this enum as evidence of alignment. It is not PLND_YAW_ALIGN
# mounting calibration or GPS course/COG selection for MOOS navigation.
# No flight-control command, new yaw authority or custom recovery patch is
# added; the existing optional pre-LAND alignment stays separately controlled.
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#wp-yaw-behavior
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/rtl-mode.html
# Source: ArduCopter/Parameters.cpp, autoyaw.cpp (AutoYaw::default_mode),
# mode_rtl.cpp (loiterathome_start), reviewed companion Copter 4.7.1.
WP_YAW_BEHAVIOR   2

# RALLY_INCL_HOME -- include Home in RTL rally destination competition (enum).
# Upstream choices: "0:DoNotIncludeHome,1:IncludeHome"; no physical units.
# User explicitly selected 0: choose a valid eligible rally even if Home is
# closer, rather than treating Home as another acceptable landing destination.
# With 1, Home can win the distance comparison. The mission freezes/uploads
# one rally via its native ACK/readback transaction; this parameter does not
# create that point, prove its validity, or grant flight/RTL authority.
# Keep the existing verified-rally admission requirement. Do NOT hand-set
# RALLY_TOTAL to pretend that rally storage/upload has succeeded.
# Limitation: upstream still falls back to Home if no valid eligible rally is
# found, including rejection by a nonzero RALLY_LIMIT_KM. Thus 0 excludes Home
# from competition, not every possible fallback; approved RALLY_LIMIT_KM=0
# below disables distance rejection. Missing/invalid rally storage during flight
# is not repaired by either flag.
# Excluding Home as a destination does not remove Home as the RTL_ALT_TYPE=0
# altitude reference or as the reference for the frozen rally's altitude.
# This is upstream selection behavior, not a custom no-Home fail-safe patch
# or the pending one-shot precision-retry-exhaustion -> RTL policy.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/common-rally-points.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rally-incl-home
# Source: libraries/AP_Rally/AP_Rally.cpp, var_info/find_nearest_rally_point/
# calc_best_rally_or_home_location in reviewed companion ArduPilot 4.7.1.
RALLY_INCL_HOME   0

# RALLY_LIMIT_KM -- maximum permitted distance to the nearest rally, kilometres.
# Upstream metadata: "If this is set to 0 then the closest rally point is
# always used." User-approved 0 disables distance-based rally rejection; it
# does not mean zero travel distance or force the vehicle to land where it is.
# Combined with RALLY_INCL_HOME=0, select an eligible native rally rather than
# rejecting it because it is far away or preferring a closer Home destination.
# With a positive limit, the reviewed native find_nearest_rally_point rejects
# a nearest rally beyond limit*1000 m, allowing fallback to Home. Zero disables
# that test. No valid rally still permits upstream Home fallback: this is not
# an unconditional no-Home failsafe and does not manufacture rally validity.
# Removing the limit also removes protection against distant leftover rallies.
# Retain the existing one task-frozen point, native ACK/readback transaction and
# verified-rally admission; never use retained/unverified storage as readiness.
# This entry does not upload/clear rally storage, hand-set RALLY_TOTAL, change
# the frozen point/altitude, grant flight authority or implement retry -> RTL.
# This is upstream selection behavior, not a custom firmware patch.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/common-rally-points.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#rally-limit-km
# Source: libraries/AP_Rally/AP_Rally.cpp, var_info/find_nearest_rally_point/
# calc_best_rally_or_home_location in reviewed companion ArduPilot 4.7.1.
RALLY_LIMIT_KM    0

# PLND_ENABLED -- enable ArduPilot's precision-landing subsystem (boolean).
# Official Quick Start: "Set PLND_ENABLED = 1 and reboot to expose parameters."
# With 1, a detected target can guide the final descent in LAND. This does NOT
# enter LAND, arm the aircraft, or guarantee target acquisition. The coordinator
# must still check the target actually received by pArduBridge before AUTOLAND.
# With 0, the generic AUTOLAND command can still land, but without this precision
# backend. This startup overlay enables the subsystem before the flight begins.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html#quick-start
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-enabled
PLND_ENABLED      1

# PLND_TYPE -- select the precision-landing observation backend (enum).
# Official choices: 0 disables; 1 MAVLink LANDING_TARGET; 2 IR-LOCK;
# 3 Gazebo simulation; 4 native SITL simulation. Use 1 even though this is SITL:
# pMavlinkLandingTarget supplies real MAVLink messages on its independent link.
# It reports a position-valid BODY_FRD vector to PEARL's geographic deck center,
# using full native aircraft attitude/estimated position and the same MSL deck
# altitude as the physical simulator; no yaw-only body-frame approximation.
# Selecting 4 would test ArduPilot's built-in synthetic sensor instead of the
# mission's external observation stream and bridge-reported target checks.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html#quick-start
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-type
# Message: https://mavlink.io/en/messages/common.html#LANDING_TARGET
PLND_TYPE         1

# PLND_OPTIONS -- precision-landing behavior bitmask, not a boolean.
# Official instruction: "Set PLND_OPTIONS bit 0 if landing on a moving target."
# Value 1 = 2^0: declare a moving target rather than the default stationary-target
# assumption. PEARL moves during approach and may drift while stationkeeping.
# Bit 1 (value 2, continue precision landing after manual pilot reposition) and
# bit 2 (value 4, faster final descent) remain unset. Thus this setting does not
# enable either feature; ArduPilot can reduce final descent speed for accuracy.
# The mission normally has no RC input; this option does not create one.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html#operation
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-options
PLND_OPTIONS      1

# PLND_STRICT -- response to a missing/lost precision target (enum).
# Official mode 2: "perform landing retries until PLND_RET_MAX is reached,
# then hover instead of landing." Mode 0 permits ordinary landing; mode 1
# retries, then permits ordinary landing. User reaffirmed 2 alongside
# RTL_ALT_FINAL_M=0; choose 2 to avoid silently treating
# target loss over a boat as permission for an unobserved off-deck touchdown.
# Retry count is PLND_RET_MAX=3, timeout PLND_TIMEOUT=1 and geometry
# PLND_RET_BEHAVE=1 below. No immediate hover on every loss is promised.
# The explicitly selected altitude exceptions are documented below. They need
# healthy downward rangefinder data: upstream treats missing/unhealthy height
# as OUT_OF_RANGE and permits descent, even with strictness 2. This is NOT an
# unconditional fail-closed landing guarantee. MOOS gates the initial LAND
# request, but ArduPilot, not the coordinator or bridge, owns in-flight retries.
# Separately, actual pilot repositioning allowed by LAND_REPOSITION=1 bypasses
# the precision state machine (including strict retries/hover). With OPTIONS=1
# this override stays latched after stick release; see the explicit entry below.
# Proposed retry-exhaustion -> RTL is NOT implemented by this upstream value;
# generic precision-landing FAILSAFE is not proof of actual retry exhaustion.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html#operation
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-strict
PLND_STRICT       2

# PLND_ALT_MIN -- lower target-loss retry/strictness height exception, metres.
# Pinned upstream metadata: "Vehicle will continue landing vertically even if
# target is lost below this height. This needs a rangefinder to work. Set to zero
# to disable this."
# User-selected 0 disables this height exception: do not intentionally allow a
# blind near-deck finish. This is rangefinder-derived height, not Home-relative
# altitude or LANDING_TARGET distance. It does NOT disable the sensor's own
# hardware minimum, cure unhealthy range data, or guarantee retries on every loss.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-alt-min
PLND_ALT_MIN      0

# PLND_ALT_MAX -- upper target-loss retry/strictness height exception, metres.
# Pinned upstream metadata: "Vehicle will continue landing vertically until
# this height if target is not found." User-selected 4 permits
# vertical search descent above 4 m when the target is missing. At/below 4 m,
# healthy downward range data admits strict missing-target/retry handling;
# target acquisition itself is not restricted to this height. A never-seen
# target has no known retry position; strictness 2 can hold instead of retrying.
# With either height exception nonzero, missing/unhealthy range data bypasses
# this protection (OUT_OF_RANGE -> descent). The 12 m sensor maximum does not
# imply valid height at a 15 m RTL return, and this is NOT an RTL action setting.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-alt-max
# Pinned behavior: AC_PrecLand::check_if_sensor_in_range / check_target_status,
# AC_PrecLand_StateMachine::update in companion ardupilot-sitl/ardupilot.
PLND_ALT_MAX      4

# PLND_XY_DIST_MAX -- precision-landing horizontal descent gate, metres.
# Upstream metadata: "The vehicle will not start descending if the landing
# target is detected and it is further than this many meters away. Set 0 to
# always descend." Documented range 0..10 m; upstream default is 2.5 m.
# User-selected 0.3 m requires closer estimated alignment before precision
# descent over the deck, instead of allowing descent from 2.5 m off-center.
# Native land_run_vertical_control computes sqrt(dNorth^2 + dEast^2) between
# estimated target and aircraft navigation positions in the EKF-origin frame.
# This is not simulator truth, MOOS rendezvous/footprint distance, vertical
# separation, or raw body-frame/slant range. Estimator/mounting/timing errors
# can affect it; this entry does not tune those or prove their correctness.
# During active precision landing (target acquired, horizontal navigation
# active, no pilot reposition), estimated error >0.3 m commands zero vertical
# climb/descent rate while horizontal target correction continues. At <=0.3 m
# descent is allowed, subject to other controls. The gate is checked repeatedly,
# so a later error increase can pause descent again; braking is not instantaneous.
# This tight gate may lengthen hover/alignment or repeatedly pause descent with
# a moving target/noisy estimate. It does not guarantee 0.3 m touchdown accuracy,
# horizontal containment on the deck, or any particular correction speed.
# With no acquired target this gate is inactive: PLND_STRICT and ALT_MIN/MAX
# still govern search/loss behavior, including the unhealthy-height bypass.
# Exceeding this gate alone is not a landing retry, retry-exhaustion fact, RTL
# request or MOOS failure timeout. No flight command or custom patch is added.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-xy-dist-max
# Source: libraries/AC_PrecLand/AC_PrecLand.cpp and ArduCopter/mode.cpp,
# Mode::land_run_vertical_control/horizontal_control, reviewed Copter 4.7.1.
PLND_XY_DIST_MAX  0.3

# PLND_RET_MAX -- maximum allowed native precision-landing retries (count).
# Upstream metadata: "PrecLand Maximum number of retires for a failed landing.
# Set to zero to disable landing retry." Integer range 0..10, no physical units;
# default 4. User-selected 3 bounds native retry allowance below that default.
# Retries need a previously acquired target/known retry location. A never-seen
# target does not manufacture one; PLND_STRICT=2 can hover without any retry.
# PLND_TIMEOUT governs loss-descent time, PLND_RET_BEHAVE selects retry geometry,
# and ALT_MIN/MAX with native height validity determine when retry logic applies.
# This count does not set those values or remove unhealthy-height descent bypass.
# Native counter is initialized on precision state-machine init/mode change;
# reacquiring a target resets the loss/retry substeps, not that mode's counter.
# It is not a mission-global three-attempt budget across LAND/RTL transitions.
# Maximum allowance is not a promise that three retries execute: a completed
# unsuccessful retry can itself enter FAILSAFE, as can a never-seen target.
# With STRICT=2 that failsafe holds rather than allowing its blind-land action;
# out-of-range classification still permits descent as documented above.
# Retry motion follows native position control; count 3 does not guarantee safe
# clearance, successful reacquisition, touchdown, or a maximum elapsed duration.
# This upstream parameter does NOT issue RTL on exhaustion. The proposed
# verified-exhaustion -> one-shot RTL policy remains unimplemented; generic
# FAILSAFE or a timeout must not be treated as evidence of all retries exhausted.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-ret-max
# Source: libraries/AC_PrecLand/AC_PrecLand.cpp and AC_PrecLand_StateMachine.cpp,
# init/reset_failed_landing_statemachine/retry_landing, reviewed Copter 4.7.1.
PLND_RET_MAX      3

# PLND_TIMEOUT -- native target-loss descent/retry time threshold, seconds.
# Upstream metadata: "Time for which vehicle continues descend even if target
# is lost. After this time period, vehicle will attempt a landing retry
# depending on PLND_STRICT parameter." Documented range 0..20 s; default 4 s.
# User-approved 1 s shortens the threshold before eligible retry handling,
# limiting deliberate continued descent compared with the default 4 seconds.
# The native comparison uses FC millis() minus get_last_valid_target_ms(),
# not a new MOOS timer started when loss is first reported. Native acquisition/
# target-loss detection has separate timing; 1 does not guarantee braking or
# retry exactly one second after a dropped packet, nor a 1 s total loss delay.
# State-machine updates and vertical-control dynamics also affect motion.
# In SITL, the FC clock follows simulation speedup, not necessarily wall time.
# With STRICT=2 and RET_MAX=3, a previously acquired/lost target can lead to a
# retry if the height/state allow it and a known retry location exists. A
# never-seen target has no retry position and can hold without using this loss
# threshold. ALT_MIN/MAX exceptions and invalid-height OUT_OF_RANGE still
# permit descent; this timeout does not fix that bypass or guarantee touchdown.
# Reacquisition resets loss/retry substeps; timeout is not a retry period,
# mission-global deadline, evidence of retry exhaustion, or a request for RTL.
# Retry geometry remains separately controlled by PLND_RET_BEHAVE. This is
# upstream behavior, not the pending custom one-shot retry-exhaustion recovery.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-timeout
# Source: libraries/AC_PrecLand/AC_PrecLand.cpp and AC_PrecLand_StateMachine.cpp,
# get_target_lost_actions, reviewed companion ArduPilot 4.7.1.
PLND_TIMEOUT      1

# PLND_RET_BEHAVE -- native precision-landing retry destination (enum, no units).
# Upstream choices: 0 "Go to the last location where landing target was
# detected", 1 "Go towards the approximate location of the detected landing
# target". Reviewed source resolves 0 to recorded aircraft position at a valid
# observation, and 1 to the recorded estimated landing location. Default is 0.
# User-selected 1 aims to recenter over the estimated pad after an off-center
# target loss instead of revisiting the aircraft's previous viewing position.
# The native stored target XY includes precision-estimator/mount/landing-offset
# corrections; it is not PEARL truth, MOOS's frozen rendezvous or the RTL rally.
# Target retry Z records aircraft height at a valid observation, not deck height.
# Reviewed retry_landing adds a hard-coded 1.5 m upward offset to that recorded
# height, moves toward the selected XYZ, then can descend to the recorded height
# if still unsuccessful. Arrival tolerances/control dynamics apply; neither
# constant is configured by this enum. This does not guarantee clear retreat
# space, an exact vertical-first trajectory, or successful target reacquisition.
# Acquiring the target again resumes normal precision guidance. Without it,
# native retry completion can enter failsafe/hover before RET_MAX=3 is consumed.
# With no previously acquired target there is no known retry destination.
# These are remembered earth-frame locations, NOT live/predicted PEARL tracking
# during target loss. PLND_OPTIONS=1 does not make the retry point follow a boat
# while unseen; motion, stale estimates or target-location error can make it
# unsuitable. Platform hold/clearance and native sensor/height validity remain
# necessary. ALT_MIN/MAX/OUT_OF_RANGE can interrupt retry handling; this enum
# does not remove the unhealthy-height descent bypass described above.
# PLND_TIMEOUT=1 and STRICT=2 govern loss handling separately. This selects no
# rally, changes no retry budget, issues no RTL command and implements no custom
# retry-exhaustion recovery. All behavior here is upstream Copter 4.7.1.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-ret-behave
# Source: libraries/AC_PrecLand/AC_PrecLand.cpp (construct_pos_meas,
# run_output_prediction) and AC_PrecLand_StateMachine.cpp (retry_landing).
PLND_RET_BEHAVE   1

# PLND_EST_TYPE -- precision-landing target estimator (enum, no physical units).
# Upstream choices: "0:RawSensor, 1:KalmanFilter". User-approved 1 retains the
# upstream default/previously observed estimator rather than switching to raw
# measurements. This selects the precision-target estimator, NOT the aircraft
# navigation EKF; no navigation source, EKF reset or readiness shortcut is added.
# Kalman mode fuses horizontal target observations with native aircraft inertial
# motion to estimate relative North/East position and velocity, predicts between
# observations and can reject inconsistent measurements. This can reduce noisy
# guidance/descent-gate changes but is not proof that estimates are correct.
# With PLND_OPTIONS bit 0 set, native guidance can use estimated target velocity
# as feed-forward. Raw mode instead returns zero target-velocity feed-forward;
# raw position still undergoes attitude/mount transforms and motion compensation.
# Reviewed Kalman init requires about 2 FC seconds of sufficiently continuous
# accepted observations; a >500 ms accepted-update gap during init can fail it.
# A received LANDING_TARGET packet is not proof of FC target acquisition. Filter
# initialization, outlier handling and smoothing can affect response to movement.
# PLND_ACC_P_NSE controls camera/inertial weighting and PLND_LAG the assumed
# observation delay; neither is changed by this selection or tuned here.
# Systematic geometry, mounting, timing or transport errors are not repaired
# by filtering. It cannot reliably track an unseen moving boat indefinitely.
# Target-loss/search/retry policy remains governed separately by STRICT,
# ALT_MIN/MAX, TIMEOUT and RET_MAX/RET_BEHAVE. Sensor validity and the documented
# unhealthy-height bypass still matter; estimator selection does not guarantee
# safe touchdown, trigger RTL, or implement the pending custom recovery policy.
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-est-type
# Source: libraries/AC_PrecLand/AC_PrecLand.cpp, var_info/run_estimator/
# check_ekf_init_timeout/get_target_velocity_ms, reviewed Copter 4.7.1.
PLND_EST_TYPE     1

# Native landing speed profile shared by LAND and RTL's final landing stage.
# User selected high=1 m/s, low=0.4 m/s, slowdown height=2 m. These are nominal
# vertical-controller requests, not constant actual speeds, altitude holds or
# touchdown guarantees. Native acceleration/braking and precision overrides
# apply. Slower low descent increases time exposed to wind/boat motion and
# battery use; validate the real vehicle/deck before relying on this profile.
# The landing height estimate prefers healthy native rangefinder, then terrain,
# then Home-relative fallback; this differs from PLND_ALT_MIN/MAX's rangefinder
# validity requirements. Low=2 m is a speed-transition setting, NOT the 4 m
# missing-target strictness gate and NOT the RTL return/loiter altitude.
# No native readiness/clearance, target-loss protection or flight authority is
# manufactured. All behavior is upstream, not a new simulator/recovery patch.
# Source: ArduCopter/mode_land.cpp, mode.h accessors and mode.cpp,
# Mode::get_alt_above_ground_m/land_run_vertical_control, reviewed Copter 4.7.1.

# LAND_SPD_HIGH_MS -- first/higher-altitude landing descent speed, metres/sec.
# Upstream: "The descent speed for the first stage of landing in m/s. If this
# is zero then WP_SPD_DN is used"; documented range 0..5 m/s. Select 1 to bound
# the higher-stage request explicitly instead of inheriting WP_SPD_DN. Native
# control slows smoothly toward the low speed as the height approaches 2 m;
# this is not an abrupt switch or the separate horizontal RTL_SPEED_MS=2.
# Does not require target acquisition to begin search descent. Loss policy and
# sensor-height exceptions still apply, including the unhealthy-height bypass.
# https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#land-spd-high-ms
LAND_SPD_HIGH_MS  0.5

# LAND_SPD_MS -- nominal final/lower-stage landing descent speed, metres/sec.
# Upstream: "The descent speed for the final stage of landing in m/s".
# User selected 0.4 (40 cm/s), slower than the 0.5 m/s default and within the
# documented 0.3..2 m/s range; this supersedes the earlier 0.2 m/s choice.
# Native readback verifies the stored request, not real flight performance.
# Normally applies below the slowdown height. With current PLND_OPTIONS bit 2
# unset and an acquired target, the reviewed 0.35..2 m target-down-separation
# precision slowdown can reduce descent to about 0.2..0.1 m/s for this setting,
# depending on estimated XY error. Acquired-target XY error >0.3 m can pause
# descent. These overrides do not imply exact physical speed or immediate
# braking, and the precision near-target rule is not active when the target
# is absent. Native landing detection, not this speed, disarms.
# Legacy LAND_SPEED uses cm/s (40 here); this file uses the 4.7 metre/sec name.
# https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#land-spd-ms
LAND_SPD_MS       0.3

# LAND_ALT_LOW_M -- landing height for slowing toward LAND_SPD_MS, metres.
# Upstream: "Altitude during Landing at which vehicle slows to LAND_SPD_MS";
# documented range 1..100 m, default 10 m. User selected 2 to retain the high
# descent farther down, then use the slow 0.4 m/s lower stage near the surface.
# Native braking begins during approach to the transition, not only at exactly
# 2.000 m. Height may use native rangefinder/terrain/Home fallback as noted
# above; missing data can make it differ from true deck clearance. This value
# does not demand a 2 m hold, disable search descent or replace PLND_ALT_MAX=4.
# At/below 4 m, native strictness can already hold/retry without a valid target
# when height is healthy. Unhealthy range data still bypasses that protection.
# Legacy LAND_ALT_LOW uses cm (200 here); use the 4.7 metre parameter.
# https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#land-alt-low-m
LAND_ALT_LOW_M    2

# LAND_REPOSITION -- permit pilot lateral repositioning during landing (enum).
# Upstream: "Enables user input during LAND mode, the landing phase of RTL,
# and auto mode landings." Values 0=no repositioning, 1=repositioning; no units.
# User explicitly selected 1 after reviewing its precision-guidance trade-off,
# preserving the ability to choose another landing position manually. This does
# not invent an RC source or itself engage an override; SITL normally has no RC.
# Native trigger requires valid RC plus nonzero lateral reposition command from
# roll/pitch. Yaw-only input does not set the land_repo_active lateral flag.
# Once triggered, ordinary landing controls follow pilot lateral input while
# descent continues. Target-guided XY control, PLND_XY_DIST_MAX=0.3 gate and
# precision near-target slowdown are disabled; precland_run bypasses its retry/
# failsafe state machine, including PLND_STRICT=2. Target observations can still
# be received/estimated, but are not guiding this overridden landing. This is
# intentional pilot takeover, not a precision failure automatically starting RTL.
# Keep PLND_OPTIONS=1 unchanged: bit 0 moving target is set, bit 1 resume after
# reposition is UNSET, bit 2 faster precision descent is UNSET. Thus releasing
# lateral sticks alone does not clear the latched override; landing-control
# reinitialization (e.g. a new LAND entry) resets it. If resume-after-reposition
# were separately enabled, neutral valid input could clear the flag, but no
# such option is authorized here. Strict protection remains bypassed while
# an override is active, so this is not a target-required touchdown guarantee.
# Enabling reposition is not a global permission to arm, change mode, ignore
# native readiness or skip coordinator clearance. Pilot yaw/throttle escape/
# mode-change policy is separate; no RC/failsafe/flight command is sent here.
# This is upstream behavior, not a custom loss/retry-exhaustion recovery patch.
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#land-reposition
# Docs: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/precision-landing-and-loiter.html
# Source: ArduCopter/Parameters.cpp, mode.cpp (land_run_horizontal_control,
# land_run_vertical_control, precland_retry_position, precland_run) and
# mode_land.cpp (init), reviewed companion Copter 4.7.1.
LAND_REPOSITION   1

# PLND_LAG -- assumed precision-landing observation latency, seconds.
# Upstream metadata: "Precision Landing sensor lag, to cope with variable
# landing_target latency"; allowed range is 0.02..0.250 s and changing it
# requires an autopilot reboot. The existing operator-selected 0.03 s value is
# retained here. It compensates only a fixed assumed delay; it does not repair
# detector stalls, timestamp errors, network jitter, or target-vector jumps, and
# the actual camera-to-Pixhawk latency still needs measurement before retuning.
# This whitespace-separated spelling is required by the repository's strict
# parameter uploader; `PLND_LAG=0.03` was malformed and prevented the complete
# ordered profile from loading. This is an upstream ArduPilot parameter, not a
# custom PEARL delay implementation.
# Parameter: https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html#plnd-lag
# Source: libraries/AC_PrecLand/AC_PrecLand.cpp in companion
# ardupilot-sitl/ardupilot (reviewed Copter 4.7.1).
PLND_LAG          0.03
